## Phase 5 - Unify sort state and sortable list/header behavior

This phase implements the existing sortable-column-list architecture item from
`docs/project.todo`. It must remain separately traceable there until its own
feature and live-behavior requirements are complete.

- [x] Inspect the workspace DFHack widget implementations before designing a
      custom component:
  - [x] `CycleHotkeyLabel`;
  - [x] applicable list/column widgets; and
  - [x] their mouse, focus, width, and resize contracts.
- [x] Record which built-in behavior can be reused and which SoulSearch
      behavior still requires a custom composite.
- [x] Extract one configurable sort-state model supporting:
  - [x] allowed column keys;
  - [x] per-column initial direction;
  - [x] ascending, descending, and unsorted phases;
  - [x] normalization of persisted state;
  - [x] immutable snapshots; and
  - [x] deterministic next-state transitions.
- [x] Migrate both Results and Stats away from their separate normalization and
      transition implementations.
- [x] Remove the result/stats `kind` branch from window configuration once the
      shared model owns normalization.
- [x] Implement the reusable sortable header/list component only after the
      state model is stable:
  - [x] model each column header as the approved built-in or custom leaf;
  - [x] preserve independent column boundaries and tooltips;
  - [x] preserve current Results and Stats rendering differences;
  - [x] preserve result submission, selection, and cursor behavior; and
  - [x] avoid coupling the reusable component to SoulSearch result/stat rows.
- [x] Migrate Results and Stats independently with focused tests after each
      migration.

### Phase 5 design record - 2026-07-14

- DFHack exports native `widgets.CycleHotkeyLabel`; this checkout contains
  only SoulSearch, so the earlier local-source search was insufficient.
- `ui/sortable_header.lua` is a small adapter that constructs and synchronizes
  native per-column `CycleHotkeyLabel` widgets; it is not a custom widget
  implementation.
- `sort_state.lua` owns the three-phase normalization and transition protocol.
  Each consumer supplies its allowed columns and first-click direction, so
  result columns stay ascending-first while Stats `value` stays descending-first.

### Phase 5 gate

- [x] Results and Stats use one sort-state transition implementation.
- [x] Persisted sort settings normalize through the same model used at
      runtime.
- [x] Results and Stats no longer contain parallel sortable-header classes.
- [x] Sort cycles, exact column boundaries, tooltips, focus, selection, and
